  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL — VOLUME CONFOUND IN CONDITION B: The three-condition design requires all conditions to train on equal data volume, but Condition B (post-hoc filtered) necessarily discards some fraction of the Condition A instances. If the filter removes 20–50% of instances (plausible even for strong models on compositional constraints), B trains on substantially less data than C, biasing the C-vs-B comparison in favor of C independently of the grammar effect. The proposal must specify: (a) generate excess instances for the A/B pool so that B achieves the same final volume as C after filtering, OR (b) deliberately undersample C to match B's post-filter volume as a volume-controlled analysis. Without this control, EMNLP reviewers will reject the paper on methodological grounds.",
    "MISSING CITATION — FineCSDA: 'FineCSDA: Boosting Document-Level Event Argument Extraction with Fine-Grained Data Augmentation' (NLPCC 2024, dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-981-97-9434-8_1) specifically identifies the long-tail distribution of argument roles as a bottleneck and proposes LLM augmentation at both sample and context levels to address rare argument roles. While FineCSDA does NOT use formal grammar constraints and uses a different mechanism, it is a direct predecessor addressing the same identified gap (rare-role under-representation in training data). Reviewers familiar with document-level EAE data augmentation will notice the omission. Add a one-sentence differentiation: FineCSDA augments rare roles via sample-level and context-level LLM generation without enforcing formal cardinality or entity-type constraints — CompSchema-IE's SCG provides a distributional coverage guarantee by construction.",
    "NEGATIVE RESULT PATH UNACKNOWLEDGED: The proposal correctly states that C-B stratified gains may be modest, but does not explicitly acknowledge what happens if C fails to outperform B even in the rare-role quartile. If this occurs, the paper's primary empirical claim is a null result — potentially a useful negative finding (post-hoc filtering suffices, grammar constraints add no measurable value), but the proposal frames this as a risk without naming it. For EMNLP Findings, a clearly framed negative result is publishable, but it requires different positioning from the current abstract. Add a brief paragraph explicitly framing the null-result paper: 'If stratified analysis reveals no C-B advantage, we report this as a negative finding: for strong instruction-following models like Qwen3-32B, post-hoc filtering achieves equivalent coverage to formal grammar constraints, suggesting that engineering investment in SCG construction may not be warranted at this model scale.'",
    "PILOT STUDY SAMPLE SIZE RISK: The pilot study samples 200 event instances from RAMS and WikiEvents. RAMS has 139 event types; 200 instances gives fewer than 2 samples per event type on average, making role-level statistics extremely noisy for rare roles. Either increase the pilot study to 500–1000 instances (adds ~2 GPU-hours, within budget) or clearly scope the pilot to a subset of 5–10 event types with enough instances for per-role statistics."
  ],
  "feedback": "The Round 5 revision is the strongest version of this proposal so far. All prior critical issues (timeline, compute, ASEE/LtGaE/Gatto et al. citations, DEGAP numbers, stratified evaluation, BiTer protocol note) have been correctly resolved. The four remaining issues are fixable and one is straightforward; the volume confound is the only blocker that could sink the paper at review time.\n\n(1) VOLUME CONTROL [CRITICAL]: Before generating data for the three conditions, add a volume normalization protocol: generate 1.5× the target volume for Condition A, then both Condition A (random subsample to target) and Condition B (post-filter residual targeting the same volume) and Condition C (SCG-constrained to exact target volume) all end up with the same number of training instances. Report the achieved filter rate for Condition B as a transparency metric (e.g., 'we generated 1.5N instances for A and retained the top N after filtering, achieving a X% discard rate'). This one addition makes the C-vs-B comparison methodologically airtight.\n\n(2) CITE FineCSDA: Add a sentence in related work: 'FineCSDA (NLPCC 2024) identifies the rare-role under-representation problem and addresses it via LLM-powered sample-level and context-level augmentation; unlike CompSchema-IE, FineCSDA does not impose formal cardinality bounds or entity-type constraints, relying instead on targeted prompt design for rare event types.' This closes the citation gap reviewers may notice.\n\n(3) FRAME THE NULL RESULT: Add a two-sentence paragraph to Section 3 (Approach) or Section 5 (Expected Findings): 'We pre-specify the null result interpretation: if C does not outperform B in the rare-role quartile, we report that post-hoc filtering achieves equivalent rare-role coverage to formal grammar constraints at Qwen3-32B scale, providing a practically useful negative finding for practitioners considering SCG implementation overhead.'\n\n(4) INCREASE PILOT STUDY SCOPE: Sample 600 instances (4 GPU-hours total, still within the Day 3-4 budget) to achieve ~4–5 instances per event type on average, enabling more reliable role-level violation statistics.",
  "reason": "Proposal is substantially improved and all prior critical issues are resolved, but a volume confound in the core Condition B design is a methodological flaw that would draw rejection at review; fixable with one specific protocol addition."
}
